    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Passes locally on bun-linux but times out on bun-macOS. The test is fundamentally fine. The issue is bun-macOS specifically. Let me check whether bun on macOS has known issues with `socket.write`.\n\nLooking back: the test already has 5 seconds total. Even on slow CI 5 s should be plenty for a TCP roundtrip on localhost. The likely issue: bun's `socket.write` returns a value the older async behaviour might require flushing. Or — more probably — bun is CommonJS-style emitting `data` events synchronously when the kernel buffers are large, but the listener doesn't process them in time. But more importantly: I notice CI gives `(fail) ... [5002.57ms]` and the `^ this test timed out after 5000ms` — bun has its OWN test timeout, not the 5 s deadline. Maybe my polling deadline is OK but bun's default `it()` timeout fires first.\n\nActually the test ran 5002 ms, exactly the bun default test-timeout. Bun's default is 5 s for `it()` blocks. My poll loop's deadline is also 5000. The test framework times out before the second iteration of the loop has a chance to retry too long.\n\nBut actually more important: the test passed locally in bun-linux. It's possible bun-macOS just runs slower with TCP. Let me make the test more resilient — bump the bun timeout AND/OR make the test not need polling at all by waiting for an explicit \"received\" signal from peer B.\n\nThe cleanest fix is to expose a one-shot `await peer.nextMessage()` style API. But that's a deeper API change. Simpler: register a Promise that resolves when peerB sees the put, and await that with a generous timeout."
      }
    ],
