{
  "type": "assistant",
  "message": {
    "model": "claude-opus-4-7",
    "id": "msg_01AG4t9fAwdVdSAGRP6JqTu5",
    "type": "message",
    "role": "assistant",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Order of events on `connectSyncPeer`: socket connects → `wireRead(socket, handlers)` is set INSIDE the `createConnection` callback, which fires on connect. By then, the server is also connected and the listener server fires its `net.createServer` callback synchronously on connect. So when peerA fires its first `transport.send`, peerB's wireRead is already active. That's fine on Node and Linux bun. macOS bun may differ.\n\nActually wait — there may be an even simpler issue: at the moment `connectSyncPeer` resolves and we call `peerB.connect(client.transport)`, the SERVER's `wireRead` was attached when `net.createServer((socket) => { ... wireRead(socket, handlers); })` was called. But is the server side's `socket.on('data')` attached BEFORE the peer's `transport.onMessage` was attached? Order: server gets the 'connection' event FIRST (synchronously upon accept), runs wireRead-on-server. The client connect callback fires AFTER. Then we await `connectSyncPeer` to resolve, then we call `peerB.connect(client.transport)`, which calls `client.transport.onMessage(handler)`, which adds to the client's `handlers` set. Then we call `wireRead(socket, handlers)` — wait, `wireRead` is called inside the `createConnection` callback, BEFORE `connectSyncPeer` resolves. So data starts flowing into client's `handlers` set, but at that point `handlers` is empty. As soon as `peerB.connect` is called, the handler is added — and any future events go to it.\n\nBut what about events sent BEFORE the handler was added? They'd be lost... but in the test, `a.put` happens AFTER `peerB.connect` returns. So that's fine.\n\nHmm, but there's another issue: peerA sends to `listener.transport` which has `sockets` set. When does the server-accepted socket get added to `sockets`? In `net.createServer((socket) => { sockets.add(socket); ... })`. That callback runs when a new connection is accepted. The accept happens before client's connect callback fires. So by the time `connectSyncPeer` resolves, the server-side socket is in `sockets`.\n\nSo all the handlers are attached when `a.put` happens. Then peerA's onLocal fires `transport.send(event)` → server iterates `sockets` and writes to each. The client socket reads → fires data event → handlers (peerB's `receive`) → b.put.\n\nThe likely macOS bun bug: maybe `socket.write` returns false (buffer full) and bun doesn't flush. Or something with `socket.write` inside the same tick as the connection being established.\n\nActually let me look at this more carefully. There's a TIMING issue that could matter on bun-macOS specifically: when `peerA.connect(listener.transport)` is called BEFORE `connectSyncPeer` returns, `sockets` is empty (no client has connected yet). That's fine. Then the test calls `connectSyncPeer` which connects and adds to sockets. So by the time `a.put` fires, the socket IS in sockets. Good.\n\nLet me try yet another angle: maybe bun on macOS isn't firing the `'connection'` event on `net.createServer` until the next event-loop tick. Let me add a small read sanity check. Actually, look at the test ran for 5002 ms — that's the bun test timeout. Even if my polling deadline is 5000, each poll iteration awaits 25ms+await get(), so by 5000 ms we're in the middle of an iteration that only completes after the test framework already yanked the test.\n\nBest fix: just override the test timeout to be longer than my polling deadline. test-anywhere may pass `it()` options through."
