        "type": "text",
        "text": "Locally on Linux it passes. The issue is bun-macOS specific. Let me check the test more carefully — there's a race condition. Looking closely at the test:\n\n1. `peerA.connect(listener.transport)` — Server transport is set up. \n2. `peerB.connect(client.transport)` — Client transport is set up.\n3. The test immediately calls `a.put()` after subscribing to `b`.\n\nThe problem: in the listener's `transport.send`, it iterates over `sockets`. When `peerA.connect` is called, the server's transport handlers are set up, but the client socket hasn't connected yet. So when peerA receives the put event from store A, it tries to send via `listener.transport.send`, but if the client hasn't connected to the server yet, no socket exists in the `sockets` set.\n\nWait, looking at the order:\n- `startSyncListener` resolves once the server is listening.\n- `peerA.connect(listener.transport)` — fine, no sending yet.\n- `connectSyncPeer({ port })` — connects via TCP. The promise resolves when `net.createConnection` callback fires (after handshake).\n\nBut on the server side, `net.createServer` listener fires when a new connection happens. Is it possible there's a timing issue where `connectSyncPeer` resolves on the client side BEFORE the server's connection callback fires that adds the socket to `sockets`? Yes — the client side is told the connection is established, but the server's `connection` event might be delayed.\n\nActually no — the order: TCP handshake completes server-side first, then client-side. The `'connect'` event on client and the `connection` event on server are both fired around the handshake. But on bun specifically, this could be reordered.\n\nLet me look at the bun runtime version differences. The local test passes. The issue is bun-macOS. Let me look more carefully — the real issue might be that `peerA.connect(listener.transport)` registers an `onMessage` handler with the listener, but that handler can only receive messages for sockets that have already attached via `wireRead`. Let me look at the listener's `transport.send`:\n\n```js\nsend: (event) => {\n  for (const s of sockets) {\n    wireSend(s, event);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat iterates current sockets — fine. But there's a race: \n1. Client connects → server `connection` event fires → socket added to `sockets`, `wireRead` attached\n2. Client `'connect'` callback fires → `connectSyncPeer` resolves → caller does `peerB.connect(client.transport)`\n3. Caller calls `await a.put(...)` \n4. Store A emits 'put' → peerA forwards to `listener.transport.send` → iterates sockets → writes to socket\n5. The bytes traverse to the client → client receives and emits 'data' → handler fires → peerB receives → calls `store.put(merged)` on B → B emits 'put' → subscriber resolves\n\nHmm. That should all work. The 5-second timeout suggests it's hanging completely. Maybe on bun-macOS, the server's connection event hasn't fired yet by the time `a.put()` is called. Let me check if there's a way to ensure the server has accepted the connection before resolving `connectSyncPeer`.\n\nActually, wait. Looking more carefully — `client.transport.send` writes to the socket. If `peerB.connect(client.transport)` registers `onMessage`, but messages from the store get sent FROM `peerB` via `client.transport.send`. The path for store A's put → peer A → listener.transport.send → write to all sockets → client receives data → peer B → store B.\n\nSo the server-side socket needs to exist. Let me verify the timing on bun-macOS by waiting for the server-side connection too. The fix is to make the listener wait for the connection before testing, or restructure to wait for both sides to be ready.\n\nActually the simplest fix: in `connectSyncPeer`, also wait until the server side has the socket. We can't do that directly, but we can make `startSyncListener` track and expose a promise for when the first connection is ready, or we can change the test to wait.\n\nActually — there's a simpler hypothesis. Bun on macOS has a known issue where the test runner has non-deterministic timeouts when network ops hang. Let me look at the latest commit's fix more carefully:"
