        "text": "Wait — there's another race. On the client side, `connectSyncPeer` resolves on the client `'connect'` callback. But `peerB.connect(client.transport)` registers `onMessage` after that. If the server already has the socket and immediately writes data when connect fires (e.g., from buffered events), the client's `wireRead` is in place but no handlers are registered yet for `peerB`. That said, looking at `wireRead`, it parses lines and fires handlers — if `handlers` is empty at that moment, the events are dropped silently.\n\nActually wait — `client.transport.onMessage` is what `peerB.connect` calls. The handlers Set is shared via the closure of `wireRead`. So when `peerB.connect` registers a handler, future events will fire it. But what about events that arrive during the brief window between `connectSyncPeer` resolving and `peerB.connect` running? Those events will be parsed and the handlers loop runs over an empty set — the events are gone.\n\nIn this test the order is:\n1. `peerA.connect(listener.transport)` \n2. `connectSyncPeer({ port })` resolves\n3. `peerB.connect(client.transport)` \n4. `await a.put(...)` \n\nSo by the time anything is sent (step 4), peerB's handler is already registered. That should be fine on the client side.\n\nBut for the listener side (server): peerA registers `onMessage` immediately. The server-side handlers are also a shared Set. When the client connects later, `wireRead(socket, handlers)` is called passing the same Set. So all good.\n\nActually I think my buffering fix is correct. Let me also consider the client-side: `connectSyncPeer` resolves once client `'connect'` event fires, which happens after the TCP handshake completes. By then the server has also added the socket to its `sockets` set (the `connection` event runs synchronously when the OS reports a new connection — but on bun/macOS this might be asynchronous and out-of-order with the client `connect`).\n\nActually scratch that — the server's `connection` event might NOT have fired by the time the client's `connect` event fires. That's the bug. My buffering fix handles this. Let me verify by running locally:"
      }
    ],
